@momomo Workaround is literally mimick Claude Coude. Specific headers in query, specific text in system prompt. Specific tool naming. Anthropic always change this a bit, update Claude Code with new changes, but other tools can't follow. 

From a business perspective, Anthropic's behavior is understandable. The "Pro/Max" subscription is fundamentally loss-making for the company. As many have pointed out, using the pure API costs significantly more—that's the real price. This is product bundling for them, steering users toward Claude Code and their own services. Subscribers who don't fully utilize their plan essentially subsidize those who use it constantly. Anthropic can afford to do this, but Zen couldn't finance a subscription package the same way. They can only purchase tokens at the same API price as anyone else, and this model only works if there are enough subscribers who don't max out their quota to cover the heavy users—or if you have investors backing you.

**Pro tip** by the way: you can also use Opus 4.5 in GitHub Copilot. Sure, the context is smaller and you'll hit rate limits sooner, but ChatGPT 5-mini is free. If you set it as a subagent, you can offload a lot of tasks that won't count against your quota. Also worth setting up Sonnet as a custom subagent for specific tasks—makes your quota last longer.

